  "text": "The user is asking me to explain the causes of ocean tides, distinguishing between lunar and solar gravity, rotation, coastal geography, and the limits of simplified predictions. They've also included a reference note asking me to distinguish observations from assumptions, check units, causal mechanisms, boundaries, and measurement uncertainty.\n\nLet me provide a thorough, well-reasoned explanation of ocean tides.\n\n**Key concepts to cover:**\n\n1. **What causes tides - the gravitational differential (tidal force)**\n   - It's not gravity itself but the DIFFERENCE in gravitational pull across Earth's extent\n   - The Moon's gravity pulls more strongly on the near side of Earth than on the center, and more strongly on the center than on the far side\n   - This creates two bulges: one toward the Moon (stronger pull on near side) and one away from the Moon (inertia/less pull on far side)\n   - This is a critical distinction - it's the gradient of gravity, not gravity itself\n\n2. **Lunar vs Solar contributions**\n   - The Moon is much closer, so despite being much less massive than the Sun, its tidal effect is stronger\n   - Tidal force scales with M/r³ (mass over distance cubed)\n",
